{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Positivity","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Combinatorics.Enumerative.Catalan"],"declarations":52,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.yw_branching","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.yw_branching","kind":"theorem","line":1009,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L1009-L1019","doc":"**Branching coherence on the defined weights.**\n\n`y[n+1](m+2) + y[n+1](m) = y[n](m+1)` — the two ways of adding a box to a\ntwo-row shape, summing to the weight one level down. This is\n`GramSignature.mu_triplet_eq_branch` / `mu_singlet_eq_branch` generalised to\nevery level of the tower, and it is what `hbranch` of `vanishing_step` asks for\nonce the shapes are indexed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.qint_three_term","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.qint_three_term","kind":"theorem","line":997,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L997-L1006","doc":"**`[m+2]_q + [m]_q = [2]_q · [m+1]_q`, on the defined `qint`.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.mul_div_pow_succ'","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.mul_div_pow_succ'","kind":"theorem","line":989,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L989-L994","doc":"The same with the extra factor on the right, for goals that come out of\n`ring_nf` in the other orientation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.mul_div_pow_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.mul_div_pow_succ","kind":"theorem","line":967,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L967-L986","doc":"**One factor cancelled against a power — the tower's recurring shape.**\n\n`c · x / c^(n+1) = x / c^n`. Every level of the tower divides by `[2]_qⁿ`, and\nevery branching step produces one extra factor of `[2]_q` upstairs, so this is\nthe cancellation that appears each time.\n\n**Do not reach for `field_simp` here.** Under a *variable* exponent it rewrites\nthe base — `(q + q⁻¹)^n` became `(q²q⁻¹ + q⁻¹)^n` — after which `ring` cannot\nreconcile two different bases raised to the same `n`, and the goal is\nunprovable-looking rather than merely unproved.\n\nA named lemma rather than a tactic macro, deliberately: the hand-rolled version\nwas `rw [pow_succ, mul_comm]` followed by `mul_div_mul_left`, and the bare\n`mul_comm` rewrites whichever multiplication it meets first — fine in a\ntwo-factor goal, wrong in a larger one. The lemma applies at a named position\nand cannot pick the wrong redex. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.weights_pos_of_pin","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.weights_pos_of_pin","kind":"theorem","line":946,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L946-L951","doc":"At the physical pin `q₀ = 1.10997858…` the hypothesis `1 < q` holds with\nroom, so nothing above is delicate. Recorded as a named fact so downstream\nstatements can cite the condition rather than restating it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.yw_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.yw_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":940,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L940-L944","doc":"**…and every weight is nonnegative.** The `≥ 3`-row blocks contribute `0`\n(`conventionD_kills_antisymmetriser`) and the surviving ones are positive, so\n`hnn` holds across the board. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.yw_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.yw_pos","kind":"theorem","line":931,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L931-L938","doc":"**Every surviving weight is strictly positive.**\n\nThis is the input T4 needs, and it is also `hnn` of `vanishing_step` for the\nblocks that survive. Note it holds at *every* `n` simultaneously — no diagram\nbasis, no meander determinant, no case analysis on the strand count. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.qint_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.qint_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":925,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L925-L929","doc":"**`[m]_q ≥ 0` for every `m`**, the `m = 0` case being `[0]_q = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.qint_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.qint_pos","kind":"theorem","line":921,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L921-L923","doc":"**`[m]_q > 0` for `m ≥ 1`.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.add_inv_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.add_inv_pos","kind":"theorem","line":916,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L916-L919","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.sub_inv_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.sub_inv_pos","kind":"theorem","line":911,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L911-L914","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.yw","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.yw","kind":"def","line":906,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L906-L909","doc":"`y₍ₙ₋ₖ,ₖ₎ = [m]_q / [2]_qⁿ` with `m = n − 2k + 1`, the closed form verified\nagainst `y_lambda_su_n` for every partition of `n = 2 … 7` at 50 dps (worst\nresidual `1.34e−51`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.qint","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.qint","kind":"def","line":903,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L903-L904","doc":"`[m]_q = (qᵐ − q⁻ᵐ)/(q − q⁻¹)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.terms_zero_of_weighted_sum_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.terms_zero_of_weighted_sum_zero","kind":"theorem","line":867,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L867-L883","doc":"**The analytic core of T4.** A weighted sum of nonnegative terms with\n*strictly* positive weights vanishes only when every term vanishes.\n\nApplied to `tr_M(a a*) = Σ_λ y_λ · tr(A_λ A_λ*)`: if the trace of `a a*` is zero\nthen every surviving block of `a` is zero, so `a` lies in the radical. That is\npositive-definiteness on `Hₙ/rad`, and it is all T4 ever required. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.qint_num_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.qint_num_pos","kind":"theorem","line":856,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L856-L865","doc":"**`[m]_q` has a positive numerator for `q > 1` and `m ≥ 1`.** `q⁻ᵐ < 1 < qᵐ`.\nThis is the only new input T4 needs. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.loop_reciprocal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.loop_reciprocal","kind":"theorem","line":837,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L837-L848","doc":"**`q − z` is the Temperley–Lieb loop reciprocal.** Under convention D,\n`(q − z)(q + q⁻¹) = 1`, i.e. `q − z = 1/δ` with `δ = [2]_q`.\n\nCombined with the Markov property `tr_M(x·e_n) = (q − z)·tr_M(x)` for\n`e_i := q − σ_i`, this says convention D *is* the standard normalised\nTemperley–Lieb trace — the sharpest form of the `Hₙ/rad ≅ TLₙ` statement that\ndoes not require constructing `TLₙ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.traceRadical_star_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.traceRadical_star_eq","kind":"theorem","line":761,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L761-L782","doc":"**The bilinear radical and the star radical coincide.**\n\n`traceRadical` above is the null space of the **bilinear** pairing `tr(a·b)`,\nbut every positivity statement on this branch concerns the **star** pairing\n`tr(a·b*)`. The two are equal, because `*` is a bijection and so `{b* : b}`\nexhausts the algebra — but that line was nowhere written, and an unstated\nbilinear-vs-star identification is exactly what produced the retracted\nLorentzian reading (commit 748ba9b1). Written now.\n\nOnly surjectivity of `star` is used; no involution law, no antimultiplicativity,\nno ring structure on the target beyond `Zero`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.traceRadical_two_sided","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.traceRadical_two_sided","kind":"theorem","line":753,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L753-L759","doc":"**The radical is a two-sided ideal.** Both closure directions together —\nthe statement that licenses passing to `Hₙ/rad ≅ TLₙ` as an algebra. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.traceRadical_mem_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.traceRadical_mem_mul","kind":"theorem","line":743,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L743-L751","doc":"**Closed under multiplication on the left** — this is where cyclicity is\nused, and it is the only place. `tr((c·a)·b) = tr(c·(a·b)) = tr((a·b)·c)\n= tr(a·(b·c)) = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.traceRadical_mul_mem","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.traceRadical_mul_mem","kind":"theorem","line":738,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L738-L741","doc":"**Closed under multiplication on the right** — associativity alone. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.traceRadical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.traceRadical","kind":"def","line":735,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L735-L736","doc":"The null space of a trace: elements that pair to zero against everything. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.vanishing_all_levels_dom","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.vanishing_all_levels_dom","kind":"theorem","line":692,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L692-L713","doc":"**…hence at every level above the base**, `Finset`-free.\n\nThe `hpred` here carries an `n₀ ≤ n` guard, because the combinatorial fact is\nfalse at `n + 1 = 3`: `(1³)` covers only `(1²)`, which has two rows. That single\nexception is why the base is `n₀ = 3` and not lower, and the guard is what lets\nthe concrete Young-diagram instantiation supply the `4 ≤ card` it needs. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.vanishing_step_dom","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.vanishing_step_dom","kind":"theorem","line":670,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L670-L689","doc":"**One level of propagation, `Finset`-free.**\n\nIf every `≥ 3`-row weight vanishes at level `n`, each cover is dominated by its\nparent, and every `≥ 3`-row shape at `n+1` covers some `≥ 3`-row shape at `n`,\nthen every `≥ 3`-row weight vanishes at `n+1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.dominates_of_branching","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.dominates_of_branching","kind":"theorem","line":661,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L661-L667","doc":"**A cover's weight is at most its parent's.** From `Σ_{l ∈ s} y l = y m` and\nnonnegativity: every other term is `≥ 0`, so the singled-out one cannot exceed\nthe total. This is the only consequence of branching the induction needs. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.vanishing_all_levels","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.vanishing_all_levels","kind":"theorem","line":613,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L613-L635","doc":"**…hence at every level above the base.**\n\nInduction on the offset `k`. With the base supplied by\n`conventionD_kills_antisymmetriser` at `n₀ = 3`, this derives the `≥ 3`-row\nvanishing at all `n ≥ 3` from the pinning alone — no appeal to Wenzl's theorem.\nWhat is still assumed is `hpred`, the purely combinatorial statement that every\n`≥ 3`-row shape has a `≥ 3`-row predecessor, verified by exhaustion for\n`n+1 = 4 … 7`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.vanishing_step","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.vanishing_step","kind":"theorem","line":593,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L593-L611","doc":"**One level of propagation.**\n\nIf every `≥ 3`-row weight vanishes at level `n`, and every `≥ 3`-row shape at\nlevel `n+1` covers some `≥ 3`-row shape at level `n`, then every `≥ 3`-row\nweight vanishes at level `n+1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.terms_zero_of_sum_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.terms_zero_of_sum_zero","kind":"theorem","line":586,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L586-L591","doc":"**A vanishing sum of nonnegative terms has every term zero.** The whole\nanalytic content of the propagation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.not_conventionD_of_antisym_survives","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.not_conventionD_of_antisym_survives","kind":"theorem","line":533,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L533-L545","doc":"**Convention C is excluded**, by derivation rather than by ruling. If the\nantisymmetriser survives — `y₍₁³₎ ≠ 0`, which is C's situation with\n`y₍₁³₎ = 0.0227449` — then `z` is not convention D. (CONVENTION-C:\nKEEP_HISTORICAL -- the hypothesis of this theorem IS C's nonzero weight.)\nContrapositive of the\nforward direction above. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.conventionD_unique_nontrivial","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.conventionD_unique_nontrivial","kind":"theorem","line":501,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L501-L531","doc":"**THE SHARPEST FORM. Convention D ⟺ the antisymmetriser dies and the\nmixed-symmetry block survives.**\n\nBoth directions, from the three normalisations alone:\n\n  * `→` D kills `y₍₁³₎` (`conventionD_kills_antisymmetriser`) and leaves\n    `y₍₂,₁₎ (q²+1)² = q² ≠ 0` (`y_mixed_conventionD`).\n  * `←` `y₍₁³₎ = 0` admits only `z = q` or D\n    (`antisym_vanishes_only_at_two_roots`), and `z = q` forces `y₍₂,₁₎ = 0`\n    (`z_eq_q_forces_trivial`), contradicting survival. So D.\n\nRead the right-hand side as \"the trace factors through Temperley–Lieb **and is\nnot the one-dimensional character**\". Convention D is exactly that, and nothing\nelse is. This is the statement to cite; the rest of the section is its scaffolding. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.isConventionD_iff_eq_div","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.isConventionD_iff_eq_div","kind":"theorem","line":488,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L488-L493","doc":"The divided form, for reading off the value. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.IsConventionD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.IsConventionD","kind":"def","line":477,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L477-L486","doc":"**Convention D, as a consumable predicate.** `z(q²+1) = q³`, written\nmultiplicatively so it needs no `q ≠ 0` and no division.\n\nOffered to the Hecke-instance layer (`QOU.Hecke.MarkovTraceH2`, PR #4857)\nper the coordination note on that PR: `MarkovTrace` does not index on `z`, so\na `MarkovTrace` instance currently cannot be given without inventing a value.\n`IsConventionD` is the missing side condition — with it, `z` is not invented but\ndetermined, by `conventionD_unique_nontrivial` below. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.y_sym_conventionD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.y_sym_conventionD","kind":"theorem","line":458,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L458-L473","doc":"**The closed-form symmetric weight under convention D.**\n`y₍₃₎ = (q⁴+1)/(q²+1)²`, which is `[4]_q/[2]_q³`, `0.50540422650393080319`.\nImmediate from the mixed weight and `tr_M(1) = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.y_mixed_conventionD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.y_mixed_conventionD","kind":"theorem","line":435,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L435-L456","doc":"**The closed-form mixed weight under convention D.** `y₍₂,₁₎ = q²/(q²+1)²`,\nwhich is `1/[2]_q²`, which is `μ₋`, which is `μ_pp · μ_nn`. Four descriptions of\n`0.24729788674803459841` that were previously related only by measurement.\n\nDerived rather than asserted: the pinning kills `y₍₁³₎`, then\n`singlet_branch_pair` reads off `(q + q⁻¹) y₍₂,₁₎ = q − z`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.z_eq_q_forces_trivial","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.z_eq_q_forces_trivial","kind":"theorem","line":401,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L401-L433","doc":"**The trivial branch is not a competitor.** At `z = q` the whole trace\ncollapses: `y₍₂,₁₎ = 0` and `y₍₃₎ = 1`, i.e. the one-dimensional character. It\ncarries no mixed-symmetry block, hence no isospin structure — `μ₊` and `μ₋`\ndegenerate to `1` and `0`. So it is a Markov trace only in the empty sense.\n\nConsequence, and the point of this section: **convention D is the UNIQUE\nnon-trivial Markov trace on `H₃(q)` whose radical contains the three-strand\nantisymmetriser.** ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prf:convention-d-kills-antisymmetriser","label":"prf:convention-d-kills-antisymmetriser","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/convention-d-kills-antisymmetriser-proof.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.antisym_vanishes_only_at_two_roots","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.antisym_vanishes_only_at_two_roots","kind":"theorem","line":381,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L381-L399","doc":"**…and only convention D or the trivial branch does.** The converse: if the\nantisymmetric weight vanishes then `z = q` or `z(q²+1) = q³`. Together with\n`conventionD_kills_antisymmetriser` this is an iff, and it is what excludes\nconvention C by derivation — `1/(q^{1/2}+q^{-1/2})` is neither root. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:convention-d-kills-antisymmetriser","label":"prop:convention-d-kills-antisymmetriser","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/convention-d-kills-antisymmetriser.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.conventionD_kills_antisymmetriser","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.conventionD_kills_antisymmetriser","kind":"theorem","line":367,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L367-L379","doc":"**Convention D kills the antisymmetriser.** `z(q²+1) = q³ ⟹ y₍₁³₎ = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.antisym_scale_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.antisym_scale_pos","kind":"theorem","line":361,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L361-L365","doc":"The scaling factor is strictly positive, so it can be cancelled. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.singlet_branch_pair","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.singlet_branch_pair","kind":"theorem","line":346,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L346-L353","doc":"**The singlet branch relation, in the inverse-free form.**\n`(q + r)(y₍₂,₁₎ + y₍₁³₎) = q − z` — the `r`-form of\n`GramSignature.mu_singlet_eq_branch`, from `h1` and `h2` alone. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.y_antisym_factorisation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.y_antisym_factorisation","kind":"theorem","line":333,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L333-L344","doc":"**The factorised antisymmetric weight.** Scaling the eliminated form by `q³`\nand using `q · r = 1` clears every inverse and exhibits the two roots. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.y_antisym_eliminated","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.y_antisym_eliminated","kind":"theorem","line":320,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L320-L331","doc":"**Eliminating `y₍₃₎` and `y₍₂,₁₎` from the three normalisations.**\n\nA polynomial identity in the ideal generated by the three equations — note it\nneeds **no** relation between `q` and `r`, so it holds before any inverse\nstructure is imposed. The cofactors are `(q³ − q²r + q)`, `−(2q²+1)`, `(q + r)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.counts_are_catalan","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.counts_are_catalan","kind":"theorem","line":249,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L249-L255","doc":"**The counts really are Catalan numbers**, checked against Mathlib's\n`Nat.catalan` rather than asserted from a table. Cheap, and it stops \"= Catalan\"\nfrom being this file's own claim about its own arithmetic. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.interface_dimension_gain","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.interface_dimension_gain","kind":"theorem","line":233,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L233-L247","doc":"**The interface dimension gain, which is the whole of the C5 proposal.**\n\n`TL₃` largest block `M₂` is 4-dimensional and the bond family `span{1, σ₂}`\nreaches a 2-plane of it. `TL₄` largest block `M₃` is 9-dimensional and the\njunction algebra `⟨σ₂, σ₃⟩ ↠ TL₃` contributes 5 dimensions. So the arithmetic\nof the proposal is `2 → 5` interface parameters and `4 → 9` in the block they\nsit in.\n\nRecorded as arithmetic rather than prose so the claim cannot drift. It is a\nstatement about *room*, not about accuracy: see the module docstring on why\nthree extra parameters is not by itself an improvement under the owner's\nstandard. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.catalan_five","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.catalan_five","kind":"theorem","line":228,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L228-L231","doc":"**`n = 5`.** `(5), (4,1), (3,2)` of dimensions `1, 4, 5`;\n`1 + 16 + 25 = 42 = C₅` against `dim H₅ = 120`. The pattern is not a\nlow-`n` accident. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:rem:hecke-radical-is-temperley-lieb","label":"rem:hecke-radical-is-temperley-lieb","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/hecke-radical-is-temperley-lieb.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.radical_four","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.radical_four","kind":"theorem","line":223,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L223-L226","doc":"**The `H₄` radical splits as the two discarded blocks.** `24 − 14 = 10` and\n`3² + 1² = 10`: nothing is unaccounted for, so `H₄/rad` really is the direct sum\nof the three surviving matrix algebras and not a proper quotient of it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.catalan_four","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.catalan_four","kind":"theorem","line":218,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L218-L221","doc":"**`n = 4`.** Two-row partitions `(4), (3,1), (2,2)` of dimensions `1, 3, 2`.\n`1 + 9 + 4 = 14 = C₄`, against `dim H₄ = 24`, so the radical is 10-dimensional —\n`3² + 1²` from the discarded `(2,1,1)` and `(1⁴)` blocks. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.catalan_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.catalan_three","kind":"theorem","line":211,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L211-L216","doc":"**The surviving-block dimension count is Catalan, `n = 3`.**\n\nTwo-row partitions of 3 are `(3)` and `(2,1)`, of Specht dimensions `1` and `2`.\n`1² + 2² = 5 = C₃`, and `dim H₃ = 6`, so the radical is 1-dimensional — the\n`(1³)` block, exactly the null direction measured in the Gram matrix. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.branch_four_to_three_mixed","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.branch_four_to_three_mixed","kind":"theorem","line":195,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L195-L205","doc":"**`[3] + [1] = [2]²`** — the branching `y₍₂,₁₎[H₃] = y₍₃,₁₎ + y₍₂,₂₎`.\nAn instance at `m = 1`. Note the right-hand side is `[2]·[2]`, so this is the\n`m = 1` case of the same recursion, not a separate fact.\n\nNeeds `q ≠ 0`: expanding `(q + q⁻¹)²` produces the cross term `2·q·q⁻¹`, and\n`ring` alone treats `q⁻¹` as an atom, so `2 + q² + q⁻² = 2·q·q⁻¹ + q² + q⁻²`\ndoes not close without it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.branch_four_to_three_sym","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.branch_four_to_three_sym","kind":"theorem","line":187,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L187-L193","doc":"**`[5] + [3] = [2]·[4]`** — the branching `y₍₃₎[H₃] = y₍₄₎ + y₍₃,₁₎`,\nafter clearing `[2]⁴`. An instance of `qint_three_term_pair` at `m = 3`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.qdim_normalisation_four","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.qdim_normalisation_four","kind":"theorem","line":174,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L174-L185","doc":"**…and at four strands: `[2]⁴ = [5] + 3[3] + 2[1]`.**\n\n`y₍₄₎·1 + y₍₃,₁₎·3 + y₍₂,₂₎·2 = 1`, with `dim (4) = 1`, `dim (3,1) = 3`,\n`dim (2,2) = 2`. The three-row and four-row blocks contribute nothing because\ntheir weights are zero — which is precisely why the count closes on `1 + 3 + 2`\nshapes rather than all five partitions of 4. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.qdim_normalisation_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.qdim_normalisation_three","kind":"theorem","line":160,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L160-L172","doc":"**The `SU(2)` Markov trace normalises at three strands: `[2]³ = [4] + 2[2]`.**\n\nThis is `tr_M(1) = 1` with the closed-form weights: `y₍₃₎·1 + y₍₂,₁₎·2 = 1`\nmeans `[4] + 2[2] = [2]³`, the multiplicities `1, 2` being `dim (3)` and\n`dim (2,1)`. Equivalently it is the `q`-dimension of `V^⊗3` for the 2-dimensional\n`V`, decomposed as `V₃ ⊕ 2 V₁`.\n\nWritten on `q` and `q⁻¹` with the `q`-integers expanded; `field_simp` supplies\n`q · q⁻¹ = 1` and `ring` closes it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.markov_branching_general","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.markov_branching_general","kind":"theorem","line":133,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L133-L152","doc":"**Branching coherence of the Markov trace, at every level of the tower.**\n\n`y₍ₙ₊₁₋ₖ,ₖ₎ + y₍ₙ₋ₖ,ₖ₊₁₎ = y₍ₙ₋ₖ,ₖ₎` — the two ways of adding a box to a\ntwo-row Young diagram, summing to the weight one level down. With the closed\nform `y = [m]/[2]ⁿ` and `m = n − 2k + 1` this is exactly the recursion above\ndivided through by `[2]ⁿ⁺¹`.\n\nStated with `D = [2] = q + r` and the three numerators abstracted, so it is\nvisibly the same identity: nothing about `SU(2)`, about `q₀`, or about the\nconvention enters.\n\n`D ≠ 0` is genuinely needed, not defensive: `q · r = 1` together with `q + r = 0`\nforces `q² = −1`, which no ordered field admits but `ℂ` does, and there the\nstatement is false (`0 = (Xq − Yr)/P`). At the physical `q₀ > 0`,\n`D = q + q⁻¹ ≥ 2`. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:markov-branching-three-term","label":"prop:markov-branching-three-term","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/markov-branching-three-term.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.qint_three_term_pair","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower.qint_three_term_pair","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/TemperleyLiebTower.lean#L112-L125","doc":"**The three-term `q`-integer recursion, `[m+2] + [m] = [2]·[m+1]`.**\n\nStated on a formal inverse pair `q · r = 1` with `X = qᵐ`, `Y = rᵐ` abstracted,\nso there is no natural-number subtraction, no `q⁻¹`, and no division: the whole\ncontent is one `linear_combination` against `hqr`. Every branching identity in\nthis file and in `GramSignature` is an instance of it.\n\nReading the substitution: `[m]_q = (qᵐ − q⁻ᵐ)/(q − q⁻¹)`, so with `X = qᵐ` and\n`Y = rᵐ` the numerator of `[m+2]` is `X q² − Y r²`, of `[m]` is `X − Y`, and of\n`[m+1]` is `X q − Y r`. The common denominator `q − q⁻¹` cancels, which is why\nit never appears. ","signatureChunk":0}]}